In addition to any and all powers enumerated in the City of Sparta Municipal Code, the mayor as the chief executive of the city, shall have further emergency powers as follows:
A. The mayor shall have the right to declare an emergency to exist when, in his opinion, one or more of the following conditions exists:
1. That there is extreme likelihood of danger or destruction of life or property due to unusual conditions;
2. Unusual or extreme weather conditions, making use of city streets or areas difficult or impossible;
3. Civil unrest, commotion or uprising is imminent or exists;
4. There is a stoppage or loss of electrical power affecting a major portion of the city.
B. After declaration of such emergency the mayor, as chief executive, shall have the right to exercise any or all of the following powers:
1. The power to use employees of the city to assist in the safety and preservation of life, limb and property of citizenry of Sparta;
2. The power to close streets and sidewalks and to delineate areas within the city wherein an emergency exists;
3. The power to impose emergency curfew regulations;
4. The power to close business establishments within the affected area;
5. The power to close any and all municipally owned buildings and other facilities to the use of the general public;
6. The power to do any and all acts necessary and incidental to the preservation of life, limb and property of the citizenry of Sparta;
7. Except as otherwise provided in subsection (B)(6) of this section, the powers assumed by the mayor after he or she has declared a state of emergency pursuant to this chapter shall be effective for a period of seventy-two (72) hours. At the conclusion of the seventy-two-hour period, the mayor may make a second declaration of a state of emergency if the mayor determines that the state of emergency is continuing. Under such circumstances, the powers granted the mayor may be exercised for an additional seventy-two-hour period. However, at the expiration of a second declared period of emergency under this chapter, the powers granted the mayor to address that state of emergency shall cease, and the mayor may not make a third declaration of a state of emergency to address the same situation;
8. No emergency period shall extend beyond the next regular, special or called meeting of the city council.
C. The declaration of an emergency as to a portion of the city shall specify with exactness the area in which the emergency is declared to exist.
(Amended during 1997 codification; Ord. 155B (part), 1967)

